/-!
# Finite-support polynomial presentations in a separable tensor square

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 0C2X, `lemma-separable-algebraic-diagonal`,
the finite primitive-element calculation and the union of finite subextensions.
(blueprint: lem-trf-separable-tensor-polynomial-presentation).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v

open scoped TensorProduct BigOperators

namespace Algebra

/-- Every tensor in the square of a separable algebraic extension has a finite expansion
whose right factors generate a finite separable simple subextension. Polynomial
representatives in its primitive element give the four stated evaluation identities.
The tensor evaluation uses the canonical left inclusion on coefficients. The index
`n : ℕ` permits the empty sum: for `x = 0`, one may take `n = 0`, `E = ⊥`, and `α = 0`.

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 0C2X, `lemma-separable-algebraic-diagonal`.
(blueprint: lem-trf-separable-tensor-polynomial-presentation).

Proof:
1. Pure tensors generate the tensor product (def-weil-pbc-module-tensor-product).
   Absorb base-field scalars in the left factors to obtain a finite sum. The field
   generated by the right factors is finite-dimensional by
   lem-weil-pbc-algebraic-generators-finite-extension. Its elements remain separable
   over the base field, since their minimal polynomials agree with those in L.
2. Choose a primitive element by lem-weil-pbc-finite-separable-extension-primitive-element.
   The power basis of lem-weil-pbc-simple-algebraic-extension-power-basis writes
   each right factor as the value of a polynomial over k at this element.
3. Its minimal polynomial P is separable. Map a Bezout identity for P and its
   derivative coefficientwise to L. Mapping commutes with the derivative, so the
   mapped polynomial is separable too, and its value at the primitive element is zero.
4. For t = 1 tensor α, the tensor algebra multiplication and balancing relation give
   t^j = 1 tensor α^j and the value of a mapped polynomial g at t is 1 tensor g(α).
   Thus the mapped P vanishes at t, and the mapped g_i evaluate to 1 tensor b_i.
5. Sum after multiplying by the left inclusions of a_i to get F(t) = x. Evaluation
   at α gives the sum of a_i b_i, which is the multiplication map applied to x
   (lem-weil-pbc-algebra-tensor-pushout-universal-property). For the empty sum,
   E is the base field, α = 0, P = X and F = 0, so all identities still hold. -/
theorem exists_separable_tensor_polynomial_presentation
    (k : Type u) (L : Type v) [Field k] [Field L] [Algebra k L]
    [Algebra.IsSeparable k L] (x : L ⊗[k] L) :
    ∃ (n : ℕ) (a b : Fin n → L) (E : IntermediateField k L)
      (α : E) (g : Fin n → Polynomial k),
      x = ∑ i, a i ⊗ₜ[k] b i ∧
      E = IntermediateField.adjoin k (Set.range b) ∧
      FiniteDimensional k E ∧
      Algebra.IsSeparable k E ∧
      IntermediateField.adjoin k {(α : L)} = E ∧
      (∀ i, b i = Polynomial.aeval (α : L) (g i)) ∧
      (minpoly k (α : L)).Separable ∧
      ((minpoly k (α : L)).map (algebraMap k L)).Separable ∧
      Polynomial.eval (α : L) ((minpoly k (α : L)).map (algebraMap k L)) = 0 ∧
      Polynomial.eval₂
        (Algebra.TensorProduct.includeLeft : L →ₐ[k] L ⊗[k] L).toRingHom
        (1 ⊗ₜ[k] (α : L)) ((minpoly k (α : L)).map (algebraMap k L)) = 0 ∧
      Polynomial.eval₂
        (Algebra.TensorProduct.includeLeft : L →ₐ[k] L ⊗[k] L).toRingHom
        (1 ⊗ₜ[k] (α : L))
        (∑ i, Polynomial.C (a i) * (g i).map (algebraMap k L)) = x ∧
      Polynomial.eval (α : L)
        (∑ i, Polynomial.C (a i) * (g i).map (algebraMap k L)) =
        Algebra.TensorProduct.productMap (AlgHom.id k L) (AlgHom.id k L) x := by
  sorry

end Algebra
